WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.540
- [Announcer] Some of the
biggest stars in Hollywood

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:05.850
are some of the smallest
creatures on the planet.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:10.120
Real live insects like spiders,
locusts, and butterflies

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.460
play crucial roles in many
memorable blockbuster films.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.490
But getting them to perform
on camera is no easy task.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.380
- Rosie, lift up your leg. This leg.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.250
Come on.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.400
Oh see, she did this one.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.670
Oh, there she goes.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.470
And that's just a little trick
of putting a little pressure

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.620
on her back leg that causes
her front leg to come up.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.870
- [Announcer] That's Steven Kutcher.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.290
He's an entomologist and the go to guy

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:35.310
for all things bugs in Hollywood.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.470
He's worked with a variety
of insects on over 100 films

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:42.240
in his career including
"Arachnophobia," "Jurassic Park,"

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.390
and "Spider-Man."

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:47.130
- Understanding insect
behavior is really the key.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.670
It's not training them
because you don't have time

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.795
to train them in the film industry.

00:00:51.795 --> 00:00:55.160
When you work with insects,
you wanna corral the insects.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.570
You wanna be able to control them.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.310
- [Announcer] He's used a
number of different techniques

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.510
to get the bugs to move
in the right direction

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.860
involving lights, air, and temperature.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.800
- Let's say you're a bug.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.920
How can I make you move?

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:13.920
Well, I could blow air at you,
like 60 miles an hour air.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:15.900
You're going to move.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.470
I could light a little fire under you

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:21.500
or set you on something that's really hot.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.340
You're gonna get up out
of your seat and move.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.710
I could chill down the room.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:28.620
- [Announcer] For
example, let's take a look

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.500
at one of the most famous
and terrifying spider films

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.980
of all time, "Arachnophobia."

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:38.330
- The great thing about that
film was it was before CGI.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.500
So we had to do everything
real except for one shot.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.860
- [Announcer] He devised
a clever yet simple rig

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.170
to get all the spiders
where they needed to be.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.377
- They would say, "We want a
spider to crawl into a slipper

00:01:50.377 --> 00:01:51.660
"from four feet away."

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.230
So I came up with this idea
of invisible vibrating wires.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.700
Chance of vibrating wires
that you couldn't see,

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.200
the camera couldn't see.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.870
But I could make the spider
go directly to the spot

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.470
I wanted it to go.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.020
- [Announcer] Kutcher's
first big Hollywood gig

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.780
was on the "Exorcist II"
and it involved working with

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.890
three thousand live locusts.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:14.080
- There's a scene where
James Earl Jones has to look

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.400
at a cage of locusts and the
locusts were all on the ground.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.080
But how do you move hundreds
of locusts up on the screen?

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.377
I said, "Get a light, a studio
light that gives off heat

00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:28.347
"and shining against
the side of the cage."

00:02:29.317 --> 00:02:32.110
And they did and all the
locusts moved up onto the cage

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.600
to be near the heat.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.230
Then they took the light away.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:36.550
- [Announcer] In "Jurassic Park,"

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:37.910
Kutcher was the man responsible

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.450
for the iconic dead mosquitoes in amber.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.428
- So it's supposed to be a mosquito.

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:45.150
First, it's really a crane fly.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.760
And I put antenna from another
insect on the crane fly.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.910
I took a bent insect pin and
made it for its mouth part.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.630
I created the wings.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.300
- [Announcer] And he
worked with a live mosquito

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.350
in the DNA Explainer video.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.630
- So I would chill the mosquito down

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.320
so that it wouldn't move.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.590
And then I would drip honey on it.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.633
And then it got tumbled
down with the honey.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.680
- [Announcer] In the
2002 movie "Spider-Man,"

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.150
there's a scene where Peter
Parker first gets bitten.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.880
Kutcher was actually above Toby
McGuire with a paint brush.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.370
- And what the spider
will do is it will crawl

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.995
along the edge until it reaches this part

00:03:21.995 --> 00:03:23.880
and then it will hang on.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.810
And I would just tap it
like this which would cause

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.770
the spider to web down.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.650
- [Announcer] While he says
he rarely ever gets bitten

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.930
or stung, he takes precautions

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.600
to protect the other human actors.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.340
In "Roadhouse 66," Judge
Reinhold is driving a car

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.840
and I had to have a scorpion
crawl over his shoulder.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.200
So I put a little cap on
the scorpion's stinger

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.803
that looked like the stinger
so it wouldn't hurt him.

00:03:45.803 --> 00:03:46.880
- [Announcer] And there's
also a rule on set

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.250
that no bugs are hurt during production.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.370
- In making of a movie,
you can't harm a cockroach,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.240
or a fly, or a maggot.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.920
But if the fly flies to craft
service, you can swat it.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.110
- [Announcer] He can get some of his bugs

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:00.470
from pet suppliers,

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.620
but most he goes out and collects himself.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:05.740
And Kutcher's home is also
full of bugs, by choice.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:10.740
- Right now, I have mosquitoes,
crane flies, caterpillars

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.440
in my refrigerator.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.620
The world is filled with
people who do not like insects

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.920
which is a great opportunity
to teach those people

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.550
the joys and wonders of all
of the arthropods in the world

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.370
and how you can relate to them.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.627
And when you understand how they work,

00:04:27.627 --> 00:04:30.242
the world is a better place.

00:04:30.242 --> 00:04:31.890
(upbeat happy orchestra music)

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:35.581
- To make the spider go,
I just tap her back legs.

00:04:35.581 --> 00:04:38.503
And this gets her to crawl up.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.640
To make her stop, I cover her eyes.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:42.950
She has eight eyes.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.923
She's just looking for a dark place.

